answer:
The speed of sound in air decreases with temperature. At 11 km altitude in the standard atmosphere, the temperature is approximately 216.65 K, significantly lower than the 294.26 K at sea level (343 m/s corresponds to ~20°C). Using the formula $ c = \sqrt{\gamma \cdot R \cdot T} $ with $ \gamma = 1.4 $, $ R = 287 \, \text{J/(kg·K)} $, and $ T = 216.65 \, \text{K} $:  
$$
c = \sqrt{1.4 \times 287 \times 216.65} \approx \sqrt{85,400} \approx 292 \, \text{m/s}
$$  
However, precise calculation yields:  
$$
1.4 \times 287 = 401.8 \, \text{J/kg}  
401.8 \times 216.65 = 87,085.57 \, \text{J/kg}  
\sqrt{87,085.57} \approx 295.1 \, \text{m/s}
$$  
Adjusting for rounding and exact constants, the most accurate value is approximately **295 m/s**.  

Comparison: lower  
Estimate: 295
